==German==

===Etymology===
The prefix {{m|de|ge-}} was originally a marker of the [[perfective aspect]]. As such it was principally not added to verbs like {{m|de||werden}} that were by definition (mostly) perfective. (Remnants of this distinction are still found in dialects; compare {{noncog|lb|[[bréngen|bruecht]], [[fannen|fonnt]], [[kommen|komm]]}}.) In modern German, all verbs with initial stress take the prefix {{m|de||ge-}}. The exception of {{m|de||worden}} has [[euphonic]] reasons, because as an auxiliary it is always combined with another past participle. This distinction between {{m|de|geworden}} (full verb) and {{m|de||worden}} (auxiliary) fully established itself only during the 19th century, however.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|de|De-worden2.ogg}}
* {{audio|de|De-worden.ogg}}

===Participle===
{{head|de|past participle}}

# {{past participle of|de|werden}}
#: {{ux|de|Der betreffende Mitarbeiter ist inzwischen entlassen '''worden'''.|The employee in question has since '''been''' dismissed.}}

====Usage notes====
* Used in forming the passive of the [[perfect tense]]s. In other cases, the past participle is {{m|de|geworden}}.
* In older German, this distinction was not always maintained, and {{m|de||worden}} could be used as the past participle for all senses.

